\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            <p>A routing table is an internal database maintained by a router (or a computer) that tells it how to forward IP packets. When a packet arrives, the router looks up the destination IP address in this table to determine the best path to send the data.</p>
            
            <h3>Understanding Routing Table Fields</h3>
            <p>While the output format varies between Windows, Linux, and network switches, routing tables generally contain the following key fields:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Destination / Network Prefix:</strong> The target network or IP address range.</li>
                <li><strong>Prefix Length (or Netmask):</strong> Specifies how large the network is (e.g., /24 or 255.255.255.0).</li>
                <li><strong>Next Hop / Gateway:</strong> The IP address of the next router in the path to the destination.</li>
                <li><strong>Interface:</strong> The physical or virtual network interface used to send the packet out (e.g., eth0 or GigabitEthernet0/1).</li>
                <li><strong>Metric:</strong> A cost value representing the "distance" or preference for this route. Lower metrics are preferred.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Types of Routes</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Connected Route:</strong> Networks directly attached to the router's own interfaces (e.g., <code>192.168.10.0/24</code> directly connected).</li>
                <li><strong>Specific Route:</strong> A route added manually (static) or dynamically for a specific destination network (e.g., <code>10.20.0.0/16</code>).</li>
                <li><strong>Default Route (0.0.0.0/0):</strong> The "catch-all" route used when the router does not have a more specific match for the destination. This is essentially the default gateway.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>The Longest Prefix Match Rule</h3>
            <p>One of the most critical concepts in routing is that <strong>more-specific prefixes are preferred over less-specific prefixes.</strong></p>
            <p>When a router examines its table, it looks for the longest matching prefix (the most specific match). For example, if a router receives a packet for <code>10.20.30.50</code> and its routing table contains both:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>10.20.30.0/24</code> (very specific)</li>
                <li><code>10.20.0.0/16</code> (less specific)</li>
                <li><code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (default route)</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The router will choose the <code>10.20.30.0/24</code> route because it is the most specific match. <strong>Longest prefix match occurs before route metric is relevant.</strong> The metric is only used as a tie-breaker if there are multiple identical routes with the exact same prefix length.</p>

            <h3>Viewing the Routing Table</h3>
            <p>You can inspect the routing table on end devices using these commands:</p>
            
            <p><strong>Windows:</strong></p>
            <pre><code>route print
Get-NetRoute</code></pre>
            
            <p><strong>Linux / macOS:</strong></p>
            <pre><code>ip route</code></pre>
        </div>
        
